uties are defeasible, which shows that he held a prima facie duty can be overridden by a weightier competing duty. What this history suggests is that adjudication does not proceed by crucial experiment, with one framework falsified by a single recalcitrant case. Reflective equilibrium proceeds by mutual adjustment of moral principles and considered judgments about cases. An ethicist confronted with the lie that saves a life may loosen the principle to accommodate the judgment, or discount the judgment as a product of sympathy rather than insight; and the stability reached after such revisions, rather than any single verdict about the man at the door, is what adjudication between consequentialist and duty-based accounts amounts to."
